<section class="cp-topic" id="timing">
<h2>When is the time to act?</h2>
<p>The timing table gives, for each county, the year a sea level rise increment (2, 4, 6, 8 or 10 feet, the increments NOAA maps) is reached under each of the three scenarios California's guidance recommends evaluating: Intermediate, Intermediate-high and High.</p>
<p>The years come from Appendix F of the 2024 California Sea Level Rise Guidance, which projects sea level rise in feet above 2000 for {{ opcGaugeProjections.gauges | length }} of the state's tide gauges in each decade from 2030 to 2150, with local vertical land motion included. Between decades the table interpolates linearly, starting from zero in 2000, and rounds to the year. An increment not reached by 2150 is shown as "after 2150". It uses the median projection, so it says when an increment is reached in the middle of the range, not when it could be reached.</p>
<p>The method was checked against NOAA's own table for San Francisco County, which stops at 2100: every year NOAA shows matches, except one that is a year off. The build also asserts the San Francisco table, so a change to the method that moves it fails the build.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="gauges">
<h2>Which tide gauge each county uses</h2>
<p>Sea level rise is not uniform along California's coast, so there is no statewide conversion. Each county takes one reference gauge, an editorial call following the guidance's own regional grouping where it and proximity agree.</p>
<dl class="cp-reasons">
{%- for g in countyProfiles.gaugeGroups %}
<div><dt>{{ g.name }}</dt><dd>{{ g.counties | join(", ") }}</dd></div>
{%- endfor %}
</dl>
<p>Each county has exactly one gauge. Some counties, San Mateo among them, span two shoreline regimes, an outer coast and a bay shore. An earlier version gave those counties a second gauge and computed a "straddle" note from the difference between the two; that was dropped in favor of one authoritative gauge per county, so every county reads a single set of projections. San Mateo's gauge is Alameda, an editorial call: its South Bay shore runs 0.3 to 0.4 ft below San Francisco by 2100, so San Francisco would have overstated it.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="horizons">
<h2>Projected rise at 2050 and 2100</h2>
<p>The tables below give the rise projected at each tide gauge in every decade, and 2050 and 2100 are the two horizons that appear in most California adaptation documents and Local Coastal Program updates. The values are in feet above 2000. The values are Appendix F's own: already in feet, with local vertical land motion included, so nothing is converted or rounded. Low and Intermediate-Low are not shown, because the state does not recommend planning against them. A profile's snapshot file carries all thirteen decades for its gauge.</p>
<p>NOAA's inundation layers cover {{ countyProfiles.mapMinFt }} to {{ countyProfiles.mapMaxFt }} ft above MHHW. A projection outside that range is still stated, and marked: † for a figure under {{ countyProfiles.mapMinFt }} ft (near-term rise the map cannot show) and ‡ for one over {{ countyProfiles.mapMaxFt }} ft (past the top of the map). In practice the usable range is roughly 2050 to 2120; earlier, most scenarios are under the lowest layer, and later the High scenario climbs past the highest.</p>
</section>

