<section class="cp-topic" id="connected-low-lying">
<h2>Connected and low-lying sea level rise exposure</h2>
<p>NOAA's sea level rise mapping separates two kinds of area at each increment: ocean-connected inundation, and isolated low-lying areas that are below the projected water level but that its elevation model does not connect to the ocean (<a href="https://coast.noaa.gov/data/digitalcoast/pdf/slr-inundation-methods.pdf" rel="noopener">NOAA Office for Coastal Management, method description for the Sea Level Rise Viewer, January 2017</a>). County Profiles reports the two combined: each bar or column is the total, with the ocean-connected part solid and the additional low-lying part dashed.</p>
<p>Low-lying areas are included because they can still flood. Drainage the elevation model cannot see may connect them to the water, and coastal groundwater rises with the sea, which can flood areas that have no surface connection to the ocean (<a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-020-0874-1" rel="noopener">Befus, Barnard and Hoover, 2020, <i>Nature Climate Change</i></a>). The two sources are cited separately because they support different points: NOAA's document defines the two kinds of area and warns that the model does not resolve drains and ditches, so unconnected areas may be mapped incorrectly, but it does not discuss groundwater, which rests on Befus et al. The low-lying counts are therefore less certain than the ocean-connected ones. The same combined series is used for jobs under six feet of sea level rise in the total economy topic.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="economy-method">
<h2>How the economy figures are built</h2>
<p>NOAA's original Economics: National Ocean Watch (ENOW) county figures end in 2021 and were built from confidential Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) establishment-level data that NOAA can no longer use, so they cannot be reproduced. NOAA's stopgap, <a href="https://coast.noaa.gov/data/digitalcoast/pdf/enow-introducing-open.pdf" rel="noopener">Open ENOW</a> (March 2026), rebuilds the marine economy from public data but publishes only California and the coastal United States, saying county estimates would be much noisier. County Profiles applies Open ENOW's published method to each county. The county figures are therefore <strong>our estimates, not NOAA's</strong>, and are noisier than NOAA's state figures.</p>
<h3>Sources and definitions</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Jobs, wages and establishments:</strong> public QCEW annual averages by county and industry (NAICS code) and ownership, from the BLS annual "by area" files. The marine economy is Open ENOW's six sectors and its NAICS codes for each year, including its 4- and 5-digit codes where every industry under the code is in the same sector. Some codes cover products that are not all maritime: marine transportation includes navigation instruments manufacturing (NAICS 334511), which can make up most of a county's marine transportation jobs.</li>
<li id="gdp-model"><strong>GDP:</strong> modeled, not reported. A sector's GDP is the county's wages in the industry (all ownerships, public and private) times California's ratio of BEA GDP to QCEW wages for the same industry and year (BEA table SAGDP2, current dollars). For the marine sectors it runs a year behind jobs and wages, because BEA publishes the detailed industries later. Against NOAA's 2023 county figures for the total economy, Education and health matches within about 1% (median county), the other sectors are within roughly 1% to 10%, and Other services is 25% off, most likely because NOAA used an earlier BEA release (not tested). Fishing, seafood and offshore minerals GDP are the least certain marine figures, because BEA has no line that isolates those industries.</li>
<li id="total-economy-comparators"><strong>Comparators on the total economy Wages slide:</strong> "Coastal California" and "Coastal U.S." come from NOAA's Total Economy (Coastal) series for <em>shoreline counties</em>, the same geography as the county's own figures. Coastal California is the aggregate of California's shoreline counties (what NOAA calls the coastal shoreline portion of a state). Coastal U.S. is the aggregate of the U.S. coastal shoreline counties, which NOAA defines as the 452 counties directly adjacent to the open ocean, major estuaries and the Great Lakes, so the Great Lakes shoreline counties are included (<a href="https://coast.noaa.gov/data/digitalcoast/pdf/qrt-coastal-county-definitions.pdf" rel="noopener">NOAA Office for Coastal Management, Coastal County Definitions, May 2024</a>). They are not the coastal watershed counties and not whole coastal states. In the series, Coastal U.S. is NOAA's Total Economy (Coastal) aggregate of shoreline counties in 29 states and the District of Columbia (the Great Lakes states included), Puerto Rico and the U.S. Virgin Islands. NOAA defines 452 shoreline counties; the series has no data for the shoreline counties of Alaska, American Samoa, Guam and the Northern Mariana Islands.</li>
<li id="public-administration"><strong>Public administration GDP:</strong> withheld in every county, because we could not reproduce NOAA's ratio for it. BEA's government GDP includes schools and hospitals that QCEW counts under other sectors, and no ratio we tried reproduces NOAA's. The total economy GDP figure leaves Public administration out, so it is marked with an asterisk and the slide says it excludes Public administration.</li>
<li id="tourism-calibration"><strong>Tourism and recreation:</strong> hotels, restaurants and similar industries count only where they sit in shoreline-adjacent ZIP codes, as in Open ENOW. NOAA does not publish its list of those ZIP codes, so it is derived here: a ZIP code tabulation area within {{ em.shoreMetres if em else 1000 }} metres of the Census coastline or of a tidal water body (bay, estuary or ocean) is shoreline-adjacent, and the county's share of each industry's jobs in those areas comes from Census ZIP Code Business Patterns. That rule fits California as a whole but not each county, so <strong>each county's shoreline share is calibrated to the original ENOW's 2021 county figure</strong>: it is set so that 2021 tourism jobs (and, separately, establishments) equal the original's, and held for every other year (capped at 100%{% if em.clampedCounties.length %}: {{ em.clampedCounties | join(', ') }}{% endif %}). Tourism therefore agrees with the original in 2021 by construction, which is not a measure of accuracy. Where the original's figure is withheld or zero there is nothing to calibrate to, and tourism and recreation is <strong>withheld</strong> in that county ({{ em.noAnchorCounties | join(', ') }}) rather than shown from the ZIP rule alone, which puts tourism where the original has none; the totals it feeds are marked incomplete. Each county's share, how it was set and the ZIP rule's own share are in its JSON download.</li>
<li><strong>Total economy:</strong> all-industry QCEW county totals in the eleven QCEW supersectors that NOAA's Total Economy series uses (a supersector is published even when its 2-digit parts are withheld), with GDP by the same wages-to-GDP method and self-employed workers from Census Nonemployer Statistics.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="withheld-reasons">Why a figure is withheld</h3>
<p>A slide shows a withheld sector in its legend as "[sector] withheld; not enough reliable data." The wording is the same for the reasons that withhold a whole figure, because for some withheld values the data exists but is too uncertain to show. The fourth, <code>gdp-unreproducible</code>, withholds only Public administration's GDP: the slide shows an asterisk on GDP and "Excludes Public administration.", never that line. The JSON <code>reason</code> field says which:</p>
<dl class="cp-reasons">
<div><dt><code>weak-share</code></dt><dd>The public data hides it, and {{ em.withholdPercent if em else 25 }}% or more of an estimate would rest on the two weakest methods (steps 4 and 5 of the ladder below), which our own test found unreliable.</dd></div>
<div><dt><code>no-data</code></dt><dd>The public data hides it and there is nothing to estimate it from.</dd></div>
<div><dt><code>gdp-unreproducible</code></dt><dd>We could not reproduce NOAA's ratio for it. This is Public administration's GDP, withheld in every county (see above).</dd></div>
<div><dt><code>no-calibration-anchor</code></dt><dd>Tourism and recreation's shoreline share could not be calibrated to the original ENOW's 2021 county figure, which is withheld or zero there{% if em and em.noAnchorCounties.length %} ({{ em.noAnchorCounties | join(', ') }}){% endif %}.</dd></div>
</dl>
<p>A total that leaves a withheld figure out is marked incomplete with an asterisk. Shares on the sector charts are shares of the sectors that are known, so they exclude withheld sectors.</p>
<h3 id="economy-symbols">Symbols on the economy slides</h3>
<p>Each symbol sits on a label, never on a figure, and is explained in a one-line key on the slide; a screen reader is given the same meaning in words. All of them survive a greyscale print.</p>
<dl class="cp-reasons">
<div><dt>&#8224;</dt><dd>Figures for the headline year (marine economy: 2025 for establishments, jobs and wages).</dd></div>
<div><dt>&#8225;</dt><dd>Figures for the year before (marine economy GDP, 2024).</dd></div>
<div><dt>*</dt><dd>A total that leaves something out: some sectors withheld (marine economy), or Public administration excluded (total economy GDP).</dd></div>
<div><dt>&#167;</dt><dd>A sector whose figures are withheld whole, on its label in the sector legend and on the Wages chart. Public administration is not marked this way: its jobs and wages are shown and only its GDP is withheld, which the asterisk on GDP covers.</dd></div>
<div><dt>&#8776;</dt><dd>An estimate (see below). On a bar it is also a diagonal hatch, and on a dot a dashed ring.</dd></div>
</dl>
<h3>Withheld values and the imputation ladder</h3>
<p>Public QCEW withholds a value whenever showing it would identify an employer. Open ENOW fills each withheld value from the best information available, in this order, and so does this page: (1) interpolate between the county's last and next published value for the same industry and ownership; (2) scale the nearest published value by the change in California's value for that industry; (3) scale it by the change in the county's number of establishments; (4) use the average employment per establishment (and wages per job) of the next broader industry in the same county and year; (5) the same for a still broader industry in another year. Establishment counts are always published, so they are never estimated. Two details differ from the wording of Open ENOW's method: values are estimated one ownership row at a time (private, local government and so on), and step 5 also looks at later years and broader industries when it finds nothing earlier. An estimate is never allowed to push the sum of an industry's parts above the published total of the industry that contains them.</p>
<h3 id="estimated">What "estimated" means</h3>
<p>A figure is marked <strong>&#8776;</strong> (estimated) when {{ em.thresholdPercent if em else 25 }}% or more of its value comes from imputed values. The mark is a &#8776; sign before the number, a diagonal hatch on a bar segment and a dashed ring on a dot, so it does not depend on colour and survives a greyscale print. A figure with a smaller imputed share is shown without the mark; its snapshot record still states the share and the weakest ladder step used, in the JSON download (<a href="{{ '/county-profiles/about/data/' | url }}#json-fields">what the fields mean</a>). The two parent-average steps of the ladder (4 and 5) were much less accurate in our own test (median error about 18% and 27%, against 5% to 8% for steps 1 to 3), so a figure is not shown when {{ em.withholdPercent if em else 25 }}% or more of it was imputed at those two steps: it appears as "withheld", and a total it feeds leaves it out and is marked incomplete. A figure with nothing at all to estimate it from is withheld too.</p>
<div class="cp-author-slot" data-author-slot="economy-plain-language"><p><strong>[To be written: a plain-language explanation of how reliable the county economy figures are, with the results of the imputation test.]</strong></p></div>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="land-cover">
<h2>Land cover</h2>
<p>The natural-features sections use NOAA's C-CAP regional land cover at 30 metres, the 1996 and 2016 epochs (the newest the regional product offers). Each pixel counts once, by its 2016 class. Wetland is the palustrine and estuarine wetland and aquatic-bed classes; upland is grassland, forest, scrub and shrub; other is developed, cultivated, pasture, bare and shore land. Open water is not counted as land. Development added is developed in 2016 and not in 1996. Inside and outside the floodplain, and inside each sea level rise extent, are pixel counts against the same hazard polygons as every other section.</p>
</section>

