<section class="cp-topic" id="limits">
<h2>Limits of these figures</h2>

<section class="cp-topic" id="withheld">
<h3>Withheld values, zeros and gaps</h3>
<p>Four different things can stand where a plain number would be, and the pages never blur them. <strong>Zero</strong> is an answer: a county with no medical facilities in its floodplain shows 0, and a marine sector with no jobs in a county shows none. <strong>Withheld</strong> is a figure this page does not show, for one of four reasons, each listed <a href="#withheld-reasons">below</a> and given in the JSON <code>reason</code> field. A slide says only which figure is withheld and that there was not enough reliable data; a total that leaves one out is marked incomplete with an asterisk, and a headline is never stated as an exact figure from a total whose parts are withheld: the marine economy's share of employment then reads "More than N%" (the known sectors only, rounded down, so it is always true), or "More than N jobs" if that share is under 0.1%, and a headline that would rest on a missing sector (the largest sector's share) is left out. <strong>Estimated</strong> is a figure partly built from values the source withholds for confidentiality and this page fills in (<a href="#estimated">below</a>); it carries a &#8776; mark. <strong>Unavailable</strong> is a section or topic with no data at all, and it states its reason where the section would be.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="sectors-not-shown">
<h3>Sectors that do not appear for some counties</h3>
<p>The wages chart shows an average wage per job, so a sector with no jobs in a county has nothing to average and is left out of that county's chart without comment. This is a zero, not a withheld value: the data says the county has no jobs in that sector (for example, some inland counties have no tourism and recreation jobs in shoreline ZIP codes, and several have no ship and boat building). A withheld value is different and is marked as withheld on the chart. The county's counts elsewhere on the page, such as the Business Diversity chart, still show the zero.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="marine-jobs">
<h3>Marine jobs at risk are not published</h3>
<p>NOAA's original marine economy snapshot reported how many marine businesses were in the floodplain. County Profiles does not report marine jobs at risk, and there is no substitute. Business locations, which made the original figure possible, are licensed data and cannot be reused, and the open replacement, LEHD LODES job counts, is published by 2-digit industry code, while ENOW's marine sectors are defined at up to 6 digits. No open source isolates marine jobs by location.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="jobs-not-businesses">
<h3>Jobs, not businesses</h3>
<p>The Being Underwater Is Not a Good Business Plan slides in the flood hazard and sea level rise topics count jobs, not businesses: the jobs whose workplace lies in the exposed area, from Census LEHD LODES workplace data. NOAA's original snapshots counted businesses, from Esri Business Analyst data, which is licensed and cannot be used here. The figures therefore cannot be compared with NOAA's business counts.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic" id="enow-differences">
<h3>Differences from NOAA's published ENOW</h3>
<p>Open ENOW adds five NAICS codes that the original ENOW did not use (493190 within warehousing, 713110, 721199, 721214 and 722410), and uses 4- and 5-digit codes in place of some 6-digit ones. The county figures also come from public rather than confidential data, so they differ from the original ENOW's 2021 county values. Tourism and recreation is the exception: it is anchored to those 2021 values (above), so for that sector the two agree in 2021 by construction and that agreement is not a measure of accuracy. For the five other marine sectors, which are not fitted to the original, our county figures are higher than the original's in every county (the median county is about a quarter higher), and the difference is as large where little is imputed as where much is. The checks behind these statements are in the project's <a href="https://github.com/petermodracee/ca-sea-change-atlas/blob/main/docs/DECISIONS.md" rel="noopener">decision log</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic">
<h3 id="intended-use">What these figures are for</h3>
<p>The county economy figures describe the approximate size and mix of each sector. They are not exact counts. They are built on a different basis from NOAA's original county series, whose county figures add up to less than its own California totals in the five sectors other than tourism (by 6% to 48%, because it withholds cells), so a figure here is not a drop-in replacement for an original one. The imputation test hides cells the source published and re-estimates them, which is a best case: the cells that are actually withheld are the concentrated ones, dominated by one or a few employers, and their estimates are more likely to be far off.</p>
</section>

<section class="cp-topic">
<h3 id="map-differences">Where the map and the profile figures differ</h3>
<p>The map and the profiles use different sources, vintages and definitions, so the map is a place to look at the hazard, not a way to reproduce a profile figure. Nothing here says the two agree.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Floodplain.</strong> From zoom 14 the map draws FEMA's current effective National Flood Hazard Layer, the source the profile figures use (the vintage of each profile is on its slide). Zoomed out to a whole county, it draws NOAA's copy of FEMA flood zones from the Coastal Flood Exposure Mapper instead, because the live layer is not drawn at that scale. That copy carries no date we could confirm, and its own description gives April 2015 for a different coverage than the one it now shows, so treat it as an overview.</li>
<li><strong>Sea level rise.</strong> On the map, blue is flooding connected to the ocean (the blue ramp includes open water such as a bay), and green is low-lying area not connected to it. The profile's sea level rise figures count both, shown as the solid (connected) and dashed (low-lying) parts of each bar. In the Delta counties (Sacramento, San Joaquin and Yolo) the layer shows data at every increment, but NOAA's mapping accounts for levees only as far as they appear in its elevation data. It is not a levee failure or levee overtopping analysis, so Delta islands may show as flooded or low-lying in ways a levee-specific study would not. See <a href="#connected-low-lying">connected and low-lying exposure</a> for how the two kinds of area are defined and why both are counted.</li>
<li><strong>Jobs density.</strong> The map layer is the EPA Smart Location Database: jobs per acre of land where development can occur (parks and protected areas removed), by block group, from total employment for 2017, the year its popup shows. The profile counts LODES jobs for {{ countyProfiles.lodesYear }} by census block.</li>
<li><strong>Population density.</strong> The same EPA database, from the American Community Survey 5-year estimates for 2014 to 2018 (population for 2018), per acre of land where development can occur. The profile uses the ACS 5-year estimates ending {{ countyProfiles.acsYear }}.</li>
<li><strong>Poverty.</strong> No button opens it, but the map's People menu offers it. The map layer is the CDC Social Vulnerability Index (2022 release) by tract: the percentage of people below 150% of the poverty line, ACS 2018 to 2022. The profile counts people below 100% of the poverty line (the first two bins of ACS table C17002) by block group, ACS ending {{ countyProfiles.acsYear }}. These are different definitions, not two measures of one thing.</li>
<li><strong>Critical facilities.</strong> Both read USGS Structures, but the map reads the live service and the profile reads it as of the snapshot. Zoomed out to a county, the map draws a density heat map, which counts every facility equally and so is dominated by schools; points appear from zoom 11.</li>
<li><strong>Land cover.</strong> The "converted to development" layer comes from a NOAA service whose name gives the years 1996 to 2010, while the map labels it 1996 to 2016, the epochs the profile uses. We could not confirm which years the layer covers.</li>
</ul>
<p>The map keeps no history: a link to a dated snapshot shows today's map. A figure and what its link shows can move apart whenever either source updates.</p>
</section>
</section>
